<article class="article-page">
<p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / ALFA AWUS1900 slow speed</p>
<p class="page-kicker">Wi-Fi troubleshooting</p>
<h1 class="page-title">ALFA AWUS1900 slow speed: isolate USB, driver, band, and signal.</h1>
<p class="page-lead">A low speed test does not identify the failing layer. The AWUS1900 can be limited by the USB connection, driver support, the selected Wi-Fi band, radio conditions, the access point, or the internet path. Change one variable at a time.</p>

<div class="article-summary"><strong>The five-minute answer</strong><p>Confirm the AWUS1900 is the active network interface, connect it directly to a USB 3.x port documented by the computer manufacturer, and use the driver path ALFA documents for your operating system. Then test near the access point on both 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz. Do not compare Wi-Fi marketing link rates with internet speed-test results.</p></div>

<div class="article-body">
<section><p class="page-kicker">What the symptom tells us</p><h2>“Slow” needs a baseline.</h2>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>Adapter appears and connects</strong><p>Basic USB enumeration and Wi-Fi association are working. That does not prove the bus, driver, or radio link is operating at its best mode.</p></div>
<div><strong>Local link rate is high but internet test is low</strong><p>The bottleneck may be the router, ISP, test server, or another network segment rather than the USB adapter.</p></div>
<div><strong>Performance changes sharply by USB port</strong><p>Investigate the host USB path, hub, extension cable, or port mode.</p></div>
</div></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 01</p><h2>Confirm the AWUS1900 is actually carrying the connection.</h2>
<p>On Windows, these read-only commands help identify the adapters and the current Wi-Fi connection:</p>
<pre><code>Get-NetAdapter
netsh wlan show interfaces</code></pre>
<p><code>Get-NetAdapter</code> does not require Administrator rights for a normal local query. <code>netsh wlan show interfaces</code> reports the current wireless interface, radio type, channel, signal, and receive/transmit rates. If another Wi-Fi adapter is active, do not assume Windows is using the ALFA device.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 02</p><h2>Put the adapter on a known USB 3.x path.</h2>
<p>ALFA specifies a USB 3.0 interface for the AWUS1900. Test the adapter directly on a port that the computer or motherboard manual identifies as USB 3.x. Port color is not a reliable standard across all hardware, and a USB 3 cable cannot upgrade a host port that is operating at USB 2.0 speed.</p>
<p>Remove an extension cable or hub during this test. If the adapter works correctly direct but not through the accessory, investigate that accessory rather than the radio.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 03</p><h2>Use driver guidance for the exact operating system.</h2>
<p>ALFA identifies the AWUS1900 as an RTL8814AU device. Its current product page lists Windows through Windows 10 and Linux; that listing should not be interpreted as a guarantee for a newer operating system. On Linux, ALFA’s support page notes that RTL8814AU support is not listed in the upstream kernel and documents source-driver paths for several distributions.</p>
<p>Use ALFA’s current download/support page for your OS and kernel. Do not install a random driver package because it has the same chipset name.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 04</p><h2>Compare 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz instead of assuming one should win.</h2>
<p>The AWUS1900 supports both bands. A 5 GHz connection can offer more usable bandwidth with a strong signal and a capable access point, but it also attenuates more through walls. A 2.4 GHz connection can reach farther while facing more interference. Test both from the same location and record signal, channel, and reported link rate.</p>
<p>ALFA’s advertised radio rates are PHY/link-rate marketing figures, not promised internet throughput. Do not use a “1900 Mbps” product label as a speed-test target.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Decision</p><h2>Do you need new hardware?</h2>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>No</strong><p>If changing the active interface, USB path, driver, band, or access-point conditions fixes the issue, keep the adapter.</p></div>
<div><strong>Maybe</strong><p>If the AWUS1900 is stable but your router, location, or interference is the ceiling, solve the network-side problem before replacing the client adapter.</p></div>
<div><strong>Yes, possibly</strong><p>If the same AWUS1900 remains unstable or abnormally slow across multiple known-good hosts and networks with supported drivers, hardware or antenna faults become more plausible.</p></div>
</div></section>

